      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "why": "Only family proven broadly feasible (48/56 feasible, 15/16 of the merged front). It is the 1-result/cycle backbone that supplies the high-accuracy end of the trade-off curve: with W up to 24 and N up to 28 it can push accuracy_bits well above the 2^-12 constraint, which is exactly what the HV reference (accuracy_bits ref 12) rewards. Ranges are kept wide on W/N to cover the accuracy axis, with angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding left open so the search can find the cheapest way to clear the error bound."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 24, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 28, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.55,
      "why": "This family already owns the lowest feasible power_index seen so far (3.63 vs 4 for pipelined) but is badly under-mapped (only 1/44 feasible), so it is the highest-value place to spend budget: registering every m stages cuts FF count and hence power, at the cost of a longer critical path. m is restricted to 2..4 because m>=5 almost certainly drops Fmax below the 50 MSPS floor (m=1 is just `pipelined`). m=2 is the likely sweet spot (halved registers, Fmax still above 50 MHz); m=3 probes the edge. W/N ranges mirror `pipelined` so the two families can be compared on the same accuracy axis and jointly fill the front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is throughput-bound at 50 MSPS with an error ceiling of 2^-12, and the selection rule is min power_index. Both my families are 1-result/cycle, so throughput == Fmax and the binding risk is Fmax falling under 50 MHz, not cycles/result. Prior fleet data says `pipelined` is broadly feasible and already supplies 15/16 of the merged front, while `pipelined_m` is nearly unexplored yet holds the single lowest-power feasible point (3.63). That makes the plan two-pronged: (1) keep `pipelined` wide on data_width/n_iter to map the high-accuracy end of the curve, since the HV reference point (power 40, accuracy_bits 12) only credits designs that beat 12 accuracy bits; (2) push the majority of budget into `pipelined_m` with m in 2..4, where the register-count reduction is the only plausible route to a lower power_index than the current 3.63 corner. Round 1 is deliberately broad on W/N/guards to locate the feasible region and the Fmax-vs-m cliff; later rounds will narrow around the m that survives the 50 MSPS floor and around the minimum W/N that still clears 2^-12, then spend the reserved budget mapping the front over the full ranges of whichever families land on it."
}

{
  "rationale": "The hypervolume is almost entirely set by the highest-accuracy front point: the rectangle (40 - 5.58) x (18.73 - 12) = 231.7 accounts for ~232 of the 243.8 total HV, so HV grows ~34 per extra accuracy bit at the top of the front, versus only ~6.7 per unit of power shaved. The front's accuracy range is 12..18.7 bits, but the registry allows data_width up to 28 (output LSB 2^-26) and n_iter up to 30, so roughly 26 bits is reachable - the front covers only about half the accuracy range and has just 9 points. The explored ranges are correspondingly narrow (pipelined_m data_width 16..23, n_iter 14..27 vs registry 8..28, 4..30), leaving the high-accuracy region (data_width 24..28, n_iter 20..30) essentially unmapped. The low-power corner is already tight: best power_index 1.98 sits at 54.3 MSPS (just above the 50 MSPS floor) and 12.04 bits (just above the 2^-12 limit), so there is little HV left to win there. All 9 front designs are pipelined_m and pipelined's feasible designs are dominated (best feasible power 3.47, best accuracy 17.95), so the front should be mapped over pipelined_m's full parameter ranges. With only the final 100-eval round left, spending it on full-range front mapping (seeded with the current front) is the highest-HV action.",
  "next_plan": null,
  "decision": "map_front"
}

